-- title:
--   Node annuli at the q+1 ends, q=2
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $q$ is a prime with $q = 2$, $M'$ is a nonzero natural number with $q \nmid M'$, and $\ell$ is a prime with $\ell \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$ and $\ell \mid M'$. Further, $A$ is a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ lying over $q$ in the sense that $q$ is a non-unit of $A$; write $\kappa$ for its residue field. Three function fields occur: $C_\kappa = \mathrm{modularFunctionFieldC}\,\kappa\,M'$, the subfield of the Laurent series over $\kappa$ generated by the $q$-expansions $j$ and $j(M'\,\cdot)$; $F_0 = \mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\,M'$, the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-base change of the full level-$M'$ modular function field inside the Laurent series over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$; and $F = \mathrm{fieldBar}\,q\,M'$, the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-base change of the function field of $X_H$ of level $q^2M'$ with $H = \ker\big((\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/q)^\times\big)$, the units congruent to $1$ modulo $q$. Places are taken in the sense of the project: a place of $F$ over a base field is a proper valuation subring containing the base field whose valuation ring is a principal ideal ring, $\mathrm{ord}$ is the associated normalised integer valuation, $\mathrm{evalAt}$ is evaluation in the residue field followed by the inverse of the structural map from the base field (and $0$ off the valuation subring), and rationality means that the base field surjects onto the residue field.
--
--   The data on the level-$M'$ side are: a finite set $W$ of places of $C_\kappa$ over $\kappa$, required by `hW` to consist exactly of the supersingular places, i.e. the rational affine geometric places $w$ whose value $w.\mathrm{evalAt}(j)$ lies in the set of supersingular $j$-invariants $\mathrm{ssJSet}\,q\,\kappa$; an element $s \in W$; the inclusion `hle` of $F_0$ into $F$; and a constant reduction $R_0$ of $A$ from $F_0$ to $C_\kappa$, that is, a valuation subring $R_0.\mathrm{integers}$ of $F_0$ with a surjective residue homomorphism onto $C_\kappa$ whose kernel is the maximal ideal, membership of constants governed by $A$, compatibility with the residue map of $A$, the scaling property that every nonzero element of $F_0$ can be multiplied into the integers with nonzero residue, together with a map on places preserving degrees and compatible with pushforward of divisors. The hypothesis `hR₀` states that for every Laurent series $y$ with coefficients in $A$ whose coefficientwise image lies in $F_0$, that image lies in $R_0.\mathrm{integers}$ and its $R_0$-residue is, as a Laurent series over $\kappa$, the coefficientwise reduction of $y$. Finally $\pi \in \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ satisfies $\pi^{q^2-1} = q$ and $\pi \in A$.
--
--   The prolongation data are: a field $FSS$ over $\kappa$; a regular prolongation $R$ of $A$ from $F$ to $FSS$ (the same structure as a constant reduction, without the data on places); a finite set $N$ of places of $FSS$ over $\kappa$; and, indexed by all places $Q$ of $FSS$ over $\kappa$, subrings $Sx\,Q \subseteq F$, ring homomorphisms $\varphi x\,Q : A[T] \to Sx\,Q$, homomorphisms $\chi_0x\,Q : Sx\,Q \to \kappa$ and sets $Dx\,Q$ of places of $F$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$. These are subject to the following groups of hypotheses. `h0`: $FSS$ contains an element transcendental over $\kappa$. `h1` ($R$ lies over $s$): for every $f \in R_0.\mathrm{integers}$ which has non-negative order at every place of $F_0$ at which $j$ has non-negative order, and whose $R_0$-residue lies in the valuation subring of $s$, the image of $f$ in $F$ lies in $R.\mathrm{integers}$ and its $R$-residue is the image in $FSS$ of $s.\mathrm{evalAt}(R_0.\mathrm{residue}\,f)$. `h2`: for every primitive $q$-th root of unity $\zeta$ and every $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$, the level automorphism $\mathrm{levelAutBar}\,q\,M'\,\zeta\,\gamma$ of $F$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ preserves $R.\mathrm{integers}$. `hcard`: $N$ has $q+1$ elements.
--
--   `hpkg` (the smooth-point packages, fourteen clauses) requires, for every place $Q \notin N$: the constants from $A$ lie in $Sx\,Q$; $\varphi x\,Q$ is formally smooth and formally unramified; $\varphi x\,Q$ sends constants of $A[T]$ to the corresponding constants of $F$ and $\chi_0x\,Q$ sends them to their residues in $\kappa$, while $\chi_0x\,Q(\varphi x\,Q(T)) = 0$; for every $c \in A$ with zero residue there is exactly one homomorphism $\chi : Sx\,Q \to A$ fixing the constants, lifting $\chi_0x\,Q$, and sending $\varphi x\,Q(T)$ to $c$; every element of $Sx\,Q$ lies in $R.\mathrm{integers}$, its $R$-residue lies in the valuation subring of $Q$ and reduces there to the image of its $\chi_0x\,Q$-value; the $R$-residue of $\varphi x\,Q(T)$ has order $1$ at $Q$; $Dx\,Q$ consists exactly of the rational places $P$ at which every element of $Sx\,Q$ is integral with value in $A$, and for which $|P.\mathrm{evalAt}(f)| < 1$ holds precisely when $\chi_0x\,Q(f) = 0$; each section $\chi$ as above determines a unique $P \in Dx\,Q$ with $P.\mathrm{evalAt} = \chi$ on $Sx\,Q$; for $P \in Dx\,Q$ the valuation subring of $P$ consists of the quotients $g/h$ with $g,h \in Sx\,Q$ and $P.\mathrm{evalAt}(h) \neq 0$; every nonzero $f \in F$ with all orders zero on $Dx\,Q$ becomes a unit of $Sx\,Q$ after multiplication by a nonzero constant; and every $f \in R.\mathrm{integers}$ integral at all $P \in Dx\,Q$ lies in $Sx\,Q$. `hdisj`: the sets $Dx\,Q$ for distinct $Q, Q' \notin N$ are disjoint. `hcusp`: for $Q \notin N$ every $P \in Dx\,Q$ has $\mathrm{ord}_P(j) \ge 0$, $j$ being the image in $F$ of the $q$-expansion of $j$. `heqv`: for every $\tau$ in the subgroup generated by the level automorphisms $\mathrm{levelAutBar}\,q\,M'\,\zeta\,\gamma$ with $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ which preserves $R.\mathrm{integers}$, and every place $Q$: the induced automorphism $R.\mathrm{resAut}\,\tau$ of $FSS$ moves $Q$ into $N$ precisely when $Q \in N$, and for $Q \notin N$ the $\tau$-translate $\{P : \tau^{-1}\!\cdot\!P \in Dx\,Q\}$ equals $Dx(R.\mathrm{resAut}\,\tau \cdot Q)$. `hUniq`: for $Q \notin N$, any quadruple $(S, \varphi, \chi_0, D)$ satisfying the same fourteen clauses has $D = Dx\,Q$, $S = Sx\,Q$, and $\chi_0$ agreeing with $\chi_0x\,Q$.
--
--   `hC2a` asserts the existence of a node block over some finite set $N'$ of places of $FSS$ over $\kappa$ with $|N'| = q+1$. A node block over a finite set consists of: fields $FIx\,x$ over $\kappa$, regular prolongations $Rx\,x$ of $A$ from $F$ to $FIx\,x$ and places $bx\,x$ of $FIx\,x$ over $\kappa$; an index type $\Lambda$ with subrings $C'(l) \subseteq \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ contained in $A$, each a domain and a discrete valuation ring, elements $\varpi'(l) \in C'(l)$, a distinguished index $l_0$; complete discrete valuation rings $Wc(l)$ with elements $\pi_W(l)$, exponents $E(l)$ and $E_0$; sets $S(nd)$ of places of $F$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, subrings $\mathcal{N}(nd)$ of $F$ and local Noetherian subrings $\mathcal{N}_0(nd,l)$ of $F$; and elements $cx(nd), cy(nd), cu(nd)$ of $F$. The global clauses are: the residue in $A$ of $d \in C'(l)$ vanishes precisely when $d \in \varpi'(l)C'(l)$; $C'(l_0) \subseteq C'(l)$ for all $l$; $\varpi'(l_0) \neq 0$; every element of $A$ is algebraic over $C'(l_0)$; each $\pi_W(l)$ is irreducible and $E(l) \ge 1$; every element of the inertia subgroup of $A$ in $\mathrm{Aut}_{\mathbb{Q}}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})$ with trivial tame character relative to $\pi$ fixes $\varpi'(l_0)$; and $\varpi'(l_0)^{E_0} = v\,\pi^{w}$ in $A$ for some unit $v$ of $A$ and some $w \ge 1$. For each $nd$ in the given set the clauses are: $bx(nd)$, $nd$ and all $P \in S(nd)$ are rational; $\mathcal{N}(nd)$ consists of the elements of $F$ integral for $Rx(nd)$, for $R$, and at all $P \in S(nd)$, and such elements have $P$-values in $A$; the crossing relation $cx(nd)\,cy(nd) = \varpi'(l_0)^{E_0}\,cu(nd)$; if $cx(nd)$ is $Rx(nd)$-integral its residue vanishes, if it is $R$-integral its $R$-residue has order $1$ at $nd$, if $cy(nd)$ is $R$-integral its $R$-residue vanishes, and if it is $Rx(nd)$-integral its residue has order $1$ at $bx(nd)$; for every $\tau$ in the inertia subgroup with trivial tame character, the semilinear automorphism of $F$ induced by $\tau$ through $\mathrm{arithmeticGalois}$ preserves $S(nd)$ and fixes $cx(nd)$ and $cy(nd)$; every element of $F$ is a quotient of elements of some $\mathcal{N}_0(nd,l)$, and also a quotient of a $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-linear combination of elements of $\mathcal{N}_0(nd,l_0)$ by an element of $\mathcal{N}_0(nd,l_0)$; and, for every $l$, the inclusions $\mathcal{N}_0(nd,l_0) \subseteq \mathcal{N}_0(nd,l) \subseteq \mathcal{N}(nd)$, the description of $S(nd)$ as the set of places at which $\mathcal{N}_0(nd,l)$ is integral and every non-unit of $\mathcal{N}_0(nd,l)$ has value in the maximal ideal of $A$, the inclusion of $C'(l)$ into $\mathcal{N}_0(nd,l)$, the fact that every element of $\mathcal{N}_0(nd,l)$ differs from an element of $C'(l)$ by a non-unit, a $C'(l)$-linear independence clause for sums $\sum_i c_i a_i = 0$ forcing $a_i = 0$, the existence of a subring $Bx$ of $F$ contained in $\mathcal{N}_0(nd,l)$, containing $cx(nd), cy(nd), cu(nd)$, such that $\mathcal{N}_0(nd,l)$ consists of the quotients $g/h$ with $g, h \in Bx$ and $h$ a unit of $\mathcal{N}_0(nd,l)$, and such that $Bx$ is generated by the image of $C'(l)$ together with a finite set, the memberships $cx(nd), cy(nd) \in \mathcal{N}_0(nd,l)$ with $cu(nd)$ a unit there, and finally a homomorphism $\sigma$ from $Wc(l)$ to the adic completion of $\mathcal{N}_0(nd,l)$ at its maximal ideal together with an isomorphism $\iota$ of that completion with the crossing model $Wc(l)[[X_0,X_1]]/(X_0X_1 - \pi_W(l)^{E(l)})$, such that $\sigma(\pi_W(l))$ is the image of $\varpi'(l)$, $\iota \circ \sigma$ is the constant embedding, every element of $C'(l)$ is in the image of $\sigma$, and the two branch clauses: if $f \in \mathcal{N}_0(nd,l)$ has nonzero $Rx(nd)$-residue of order $n$ at $bx(nd)$ then the image of $f$ in the crossing model agrees with $\gamma V^{n}$ for some unit $\gamma$ modulo the ideal generated by the constant $\pi_W(l)$ and $U$, and symmetrically, if $f$ has nonzero $R$-residue of order $n$ at $nd$ then its image agrees with $\gamma U^{n}$ modulo the ideal generated by the constant $\pi_W(l)$ and $V$. The remaining clauses of a node block are: the sets $S(nd)$ for distinct $nd$ are disjoint; every $P \in S(nd)$ lies over $s$, in the sense that for all $f$ and $a$ as in `h1` with $\mathrm{residue}_A(a) = s.\mathrm{evalAt}(R_0.\mathrm{residue}\,f)$ the difference $P.\mathrm{evalAt}(f) - a$ lies in the maximal ideal of $A$; for each $\zeta$ and $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ a permutation $\tau N$ of the places of $FSS$ preserving the given set, transporting the sets $S$ along $\mathrm{levelAutBar}\,q\,M'\,\zeta\,\gamma$ and matching the corresponding rings $Rx.\mathrm{integers}$; for every $\tau$ in the subgroup generated by the level automorphisms which preserves $R.\mathrm{integers}$, the place $R.\mathrm{resAut}\,\tau \cdot nd$ again lies in the set and the $\tau$-translate of $S(nd)$ is $S(R.\mathrm{resAut}\,\tau \cdot nd)$; the images in $F$ of the elements of $R_0.\mathrm{integers}$ lie in $Rx(nd).\mathrm{integers}$; there is a homomorphism $j : C_\kappa \to FIx\,nd$ carrying $R_0$-residues to $Rx(nd)$-residues and pulling back the valuation subring of $bx(nd)$ to that of $s$; and the Hasse–Igusa clause: $\mathrm{jqNModC}\,\overline{\mathbb{Q}}\,q$ lies in $F$, and there is $a_0 \in A$ such that the difference of this element and $a_0$ lies in $R.\mathrm{integers}$ with vanishing residue, $\mathrm{residue}_A(a_0)^q = s.\mathrm{evalAt}(j)$ in $\kappa$, and there is a scalar $c'$ such that $c'$ times that difference lies in $R.\mathrm{integers}$ with nonzero residue, while for every $nd$ in the given set the difference is $Rx(nd)$-integral with nonzero residue and $\mathrm{ord}_{nd}$ of the $R$-residue of $c'$ times the difference equals minus $\mathrm{ord}_{bx(nd)}$ of that $Rx(nd)$-residue. Besides this node block over $N'$, `hC2a` also requires: for $x \in N'$ and $y \notin N'$ an element $g \in R.\mathrm{integers} \cap \mathcal{N}_0(x,l_0)$ which is a non-unit there, has nonzero $R$-residue of order $0$ at $y$, and is integral at every rational place at which $j$ has non-negative order; for distinct $x, x' \in N'$ an element $g$ which in $\mathcal{N}_0(x,l_0)$ equals $cx(x)$ times a unit and is a unit in $\mathcal{N}_0(x',l_0)$; and the dichotomy that every rational place $P$ of $F$ lying over $s$ (in the sense just described) either lies in $S(nd)$ for some $nd \in N'$ or lies in a set $D$ for which $R.\mathrm{IsResidueDisc}\,Q\,D\,z$ holds for some $Q \notin N'$ and some $z \in F$.
--
--   Under all these hypotheses the conclusion asserts the existence of a family $An$, assigning to each place of $FSS$ over $\kappa$ an annulus of $F$ over $A$ — that is, a set $(An\,x).\mathrm{dom}$ of places of $F$, a parameter $(An\,x).\mathrm{param} \in F$ and a modulus in the maximal ideal of $A$, such that each place of the domain is rational, the parameter is integral there with nonzero value in the maximal ideal dividing the modulus with quotient in the maximal ideal, each admissible value of the parameter is attained at exactly one place of the domain, $\mathrm{ord}_P(\mathrm{param} - P.\mathrm{evalAt}(\mathrm{param})) = 1$ on the domain, and the annulus unit principle holds — with the following six properties.
--
--   First, for every $x \in N$: the parameter of $An\,x$ lies in $R.\mathrm{integers}$ and its $R$-residue has order $1$ at $x$, and for every $f \in R.\mathrm{integers}$ with nonzero $R$-residue whose order vanishes at all places of $(An\,x).\mathrm{dom}$, and every such place $P$, the element $P.\mathrm{evalAt}(f)\cdot P.\mathrm{evalAt}(\mathrm{param})^{-\mathrm{ord}_x(R.\mathrm{residue}\,f)}$ lies in $A$ and is a unit there; the modulus of $An\,x$ is nonzero in $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$; no place of $(An\,x).\mathrm{dom}$ lies in $Dx\,Q$ for any $Q \notin N$; and every place of $(An\,x).\mathrm{dom}$ lies over $s$, in the sense that for all $f \in R_0.\mathrm{integers}$ with non-negative orders wherever $j$ has non-negative order and with $R_0$-residue in the valuation subring of $s$, and all $a \in A$ whose residue equals $s.\mathrm{evalAt}(R_0.\mathrm{residue}\,f)$, the difference $P.\mathrm{evalAt}(f) - a$ lies in $A$ and in its maximal ideal.
--
--   Second, the domains attached to distinct elements of $N$ are disjoint. Third, for every $\tau$ in the subgroup generated by the level automorphisms $\mathrm{levelAutBar}\,q\,M'\,\zeta\,\gamma$ with $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ which preserves $R.\mathrm{integers}$, and every $x \in N$, the $\tau$-translate of $(An\,x).\mathrm{dom}$ is $(An(R.\mathrm{resAut}\,\tau \cdot x)).\mathrm{dom}$. Fourth, every rational place $P$ of $F$ lying over $s$ in the above sense either lies in $Dx\,Q$ for some $Q \notin N$ or lies in $(An\,x).\mathrm{dom}$ for some $x \in N$. Fifth, for distinct $x, x' \in N$ there is $g \in R.\mathrm{integers}$ with nonzero $R$-residue whose order at $x$ is nonzero, with $\mathrm{ord}_P(g) = 0$ for all $P \in (An\,x).\mathrm{dom}$, while at every $P \in (An\,x').\mathrm{dom}$ the element $g$ is integral with value a unit of $A$.
--
--   Sixth, there exists a node block over $N$ itself, with exactly the data and all the clauses listed above for `hC2a` — the global clauses on $C'$, $\varpi'$, $Wc$, $\pi_W$, $E$, $E_0$ and the tame-inertia and $\pi$-power relations, the per-node clauses including the crossing relation, the residue and order conditions on $cx$, $cy$, the inertia invariance, the generation clauses, and for each $l$ the chain of rings, the description of $S(nd)$, the subring $Bx$, and the identification of the adic completion of $\mathcal{N}_0(nd,l)$ with the crossing model together with the two branch clauses, as well as the Hasse–Igusa clause on $\mathrm{jqNModC}\,\overline{\mathbb{Q}}\,q$, the disjointness of the sets $S$, the over-$s$ clause, the two level-equivariance clauses, the integrality of the image of $R_0.\mathrm{integers}$ in $Rx(nd).\mathrm{integers}$, and the embedding $j$ of $C_\kappa$ into $FIx\,nd$ — and, in addition, the identification $S(nd) = (An\,nd).\mathrm{dom}$ for every $nd \in N$. The three further clauses of `hC2a` (the two separation clauses and the dichotomy for rational places over $s$) are not part of this block; their roles are taken in the conclusion by the fifth and fourth properties above.
--
--   This is the node side of the construction of the semistable model of the full-level modular curve above a supersingular point $s$ of $X_0(M')$ in characteristic $q$, in the case $q = 2$ at an auxiliary rigid level divisible by a prime $\ell \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$: the $q+1$ ends of the supersingular fibre are realised as annuli of the function field $F$ over $A$, matched with the node presentations via crossing models $W[[u,v]]/(uv - \pi^E)$, and the smooth residue discs together with these annuli are shown to cover the rational places lying over $s$. It is used in the assembly of the full supersingular chart package [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.exists_supersingularRegularProlongation_smoothPointCharts_nodePresentations_crossUnits_igusaOverS_inertia_nodeCharts_hasseJ_of_eq_two_of_dvd`](thm.html#ModularCurve.FullLevel.exists_supersingularRegularProlongation_smoothPointCharts_nodePresentations_crossUnits_igusaOverS_inertia_nodeCharts_hasseJ_of_eq_two_of_dvd).
